1
00:00:00,766 --> 00:00:07,500
On September 1st this year, the Huawei Mate 80 Pro jumped 1000 yuan overnight, from 5999 to 6999

2
00:00:07,966 --> 00:00:10,466
The same day, Xiaomi and Honor raised prices too

3
00:00:10,766 --> 00:00:20,400
If you're building a PC these days it's even more confusing, a 32G RAM kit that cost just over 700 yuan last year now runs over 4000, by overseas average prices

4
00:00:20,666 --> 00:00:25,200
The product hasn't gotten faster or bigger, but the price is now more than five times what it was

5
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:31,333
Your first reaction is probably it's just hype again, another cash grab, it'll drop back down once the fuss dies

6
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,166
That's what I thought at first too

7
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:42,266
But tracing this RAM stick upstream, I found the real reason it's pricier is that a buyer you simply can't outbid is competing with you for the same production space

8
00:00:42,466 --> 00:00:46,766
This is Pixel Breath, today let's talk about why RAM suddenly got so expensive

9
00:00:49,433 --> 00:00:53,100
To be fair, feeling ripped off is completely understandable

10
00:00:53,166 --> 00:00:59,400
Most of the world's memory chip supply sits in the hands of just three companies, Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron

11
00:00:59,666 --> 00:01:04,400
When three companies control the market and prices skyrocket, of course people start to suspect something

12
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:14,333
In fact, this June, 17 plaintiffs in California sued all three companies, claiming they colluded to cut output and drive prices up about 700% over four years

13
00:01:14,366 --> 00:01:21,666
But to be clear, this is just an allegation, the court hasn't ruled, and all three say their decisions were made independently

14
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,733
But even setting the lawsuit aside, the hype theory still doesn't quite add up

15
00:01:27,133 --> 00:01:32,366
If makers were hoarding stock to drive up prices, the pricier it gets the more they should want to sell to you

16
00:01:32,566 --> 00:01:36,533
But reality is the opposite, one maker is actively choosing not to sell to you at all

17
00:01:36,833 --> 00:01:42,000
So the real question isn't who's hyping it up, it's who's actually taking this RAM away from you

18
00:01:42,766 --> 00:01:48,000
Last December, Micron announced it was shutting down its consumer brand Crucial

19
00:01:48,466 --> 00:01:53,966
Anyone who's built a PC has probably seen this brand, it made RAM and SSDs for 29 years

20
00:01:54,233 --> 00:01:58,733
The announcement gave just one reason, to better supply larger strategic customers

21
00:01:58,866 --> 00:02:00,666
In plain terms, that means AI companies

22
00:02:00,866 --> 00:02:03,566
Why would a brand that was selling just fine suddenly shut down

23
00:02:03,733 --> 00:02:06,333
You need to know how RAM actually gets made first

24
00:02:06,333 --> 00:02:16,166
It's etched onto round silicon wafers, and there are only so many factories in the world that can do this, building a new one takes years at best

25
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:24,466
The same wafer can become the regular RAM in your computer, or the high bandwidth memory called HBM that sits next to AI chips

26
00:02:24,466 --> 00:02:27,200
Both of these are competing for the same production line

27
00:02:27,366 --> 00:02:34,733
And HBM takes up a lot of room, Micron itself has said that for the same capacity, it uses nearly 3 times the wafer area

28
00:02:34,966 --> 00:02:37,100
So why are makers still racing to produce it

29
00:02:37,266 --> 00:02:39,666
Because selling to AI companies is just too profitable

30
00:02:39,866 --> 00:02:45,666
Micron's gross margin last quarter was 86.8%, a year ago it was only 44.7%

31
00:02:45,933 --> 00:02:49,766
That means for every 100 yuan of goods sold, the cost is just over 13 yuan

32
00:02:50,133 --> 00:02:56,366
Honestly, when I first saw that number I was stunned, that's a better margin than a lot of software companies

33
00:02:56,533 --> 00:02:59,900
This is where an economics concept comes in, opportunity cost

34
00:03:00,366 --> 00:03:09,533
The real cost of making you a stick of RAM isn't the silicon or the electricity, it's the money that wafer could've made selling to AI companies instead

35
00:03:09,666 --> 00:03:13,333
As AI companies keep paying more, selling to you becomes more of a loss

36
00:03:13,666 --> 00:03:19,666
That's how Crucial ended up shutting down, it wasn't that it couldn't sell, it's that selling to you stopped being worth it

37
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:23,500
And big customers don't just pay more, they also get priority over you

38
00:03:23,766 --> 00:03:36,733
About 80% of Samsung's memory capacity for next year is already locked into long term contracts, companies like Nvidia, Google, and Microsoft sign for five years or more, and these deals usually require a deposit of over 20% upfront

39
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:43,266
That leaves just 20% for everyone without a contract to fight over, phone makers, PC makers, and you

40
00:03:43,533 --> 00:03:49,333
It's like a cake, eight slices are already taken, and dozens of people are left bidding over the remaining two

41
00:03:49,533 --> 00:03:51,866
Those two slices are exactly where prices are climbing the fastest

42
00:03:52,133 --> 00:03:57,166
That's why regular RAM contract prices jumped over 90% in just the first quarter this year

43
00:03:57,433 --> 00:04:04,333
Even phone makers are struggling, Richard Yu has said memory price hikes alone added over 200 dollars to a phone's hardware cost

44
00:04:04,333 --> 00:04:07,500
His exact words were, a company has to survive first

45
00:04:08,333 --> 00:04:10,400
So will prices just keep climbing forever

46
00:04:10,400 --> 00:04:11,200
No, not really

47
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:16,500
The memory industry has always cycled up for a few years then down, this cycle will turn too eventually

48
00:04:16,633 --> 00:04:26,200
But the signal for that turn isn't on your end, it's on the makers' side, waiting for new factories to open or for AI orders to shrink so they're willing to allocate output to regular RAM again

49
00:04:26,333 --> 00:04:30,066
Until then, waiting for prices to drop once the hype dies down is basically a waste of time

50
00:04:30,333 --> 00:04:38,533
So next time something mysteriously gets more expensive, don't rush to blame hype, ask instead who's competing with you for the same supply

51
00:04:38,766 --> 00:04:43,766
If the answer is a new buyer with way more money than you, then the price is no longer yours to decide

52
00:04:43,933 --> 00:04:49,100
If you can wait to upgrade, wait, and if you can't, don't expect prices to drop in a couple months

53
00:04:49,466 --> 00:04:51,333
Back to that phone from September 1st

54
00:04:51,333 --> 00:04:54,233
That extra 1000 yuan wasn't secretly taken from you by anyone

55
00:04:54,233 --> 00:04:59,333
You were essentially bidding alongside buyers like Nvidia for the memory chips inside it

56
00:04:59,633 --> 00:05:00,766
One last question to leave you with

57
00:05:01,133 --> 00:05:05,600
AI has been getting cheaper all year, some models dropped in price by three quarters overnight

58
00:05:05,666 --> 00:05:10,100
So why are you and your company spending more and more on AI instead of less

59
00:05:10,666 --> 00:05:11,533
More on that next time
